import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import RequestHeadersReference from '../../components/RequestHeadersReference.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="HTTP Request Headers Reference" description="Quick reference for common HTTP request headers and what they're used for.">
  <RequestHeadersReference client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why request headers carry more than "metadata"</h2>
  <p>Headers like Authorization and Content-Type directly determine whether a request is even processed correctly — a missing or malformed header is one of the most common reasons an otherwise correct request fails with an unhelpful error.</p>

  <h2>How to use this reference</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify what your request needs to communicate (authentication, content format, tracing).</li>
    <li>Look up the standard header for that purpose.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Forgetting Content-Type on requests with a body</strong>, causing servers to misinterpret or reject the payload.</li>
    <li><strong>Sending credentials in a custom header</strong> instead of the standard Authorization header, breaking compatibility with tooling that expects the standard.</li>
    <li><strong>Not including a request ID header</strong> for tracing, making it harder to correlate logs across services when debugging a multi-service request.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Setting the correct headers when building a custom API client</li>
    <li>Understanding an unfamiliar header seen in an API's documentation</li>
    <li>Debugging a request that's failing due to a missing or incorrect header</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Are custom headers (like X-Request-ID) standardized?</strong> Not officially — headers prefixed with X- are conventionally used for non-standard purposes, though X-Request-ID specifically is a very widely adopted convention.</p>
</ToolLayout>
